  "narrative": "During the 1950s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. While there is limited documentation on specific styles during this era, it is clear that natural textures were celebrated through various forms of braiding and styling. Cornrows, a traditional hairstyle from Africa, were likely popular among Black and African communities, as they continue to be today. The afro, which emerged in the 1960s but may have had early roots, was not yet a dominant style during this decade. Instead, women may have favored protective hairstyles like box braids or locs, which allowed them to minimize manipulation and exposure of their hair to environmental elements. Unfortunately, more specific information on hairstyles from this era is scarce, but it is evident that Black and African women's hair was already imbued with cultural significance and identity.",
